---
import Read from '../../layouts/Read.astro';
import Share from '../../components/Share.astro';
import BarTable from '../../components/BarTable.astro';
import BearDual from '../../components/marks/BearDual.astro';
import { WHEY_US_SHARE, WHEY_SURTAX, WPC_AUG_2026, WPC_AUG_2023, WPC_RATIO, SMPIF_CAP, SMPIF_BUDGET } from '../../lib/figures';
import { MODEL, day, cases, plant, landed, netCapital, grantAmount, skidTotal, dollars, kg, run, BASE_INPUTS } from '../../lib/whey';
import { SITE } from '../../config.mjs';

/**
 * Northern Temper Paper No. 05, the web edition of the owner's own PDF "The
 * protein leaves as water. It comes back as a bill." (Northern Temper,
 * October 2026, 16 pages), uploaded 27 October 2026 with the plan behind it,
 * a Word document kept unpublished at notes/canadian-whey-business-plan.md.
 * An eight-page cut of the same PDF was uploaded beside it; this edition
 * follows the sixteen.
 *
 * THE NUMBER. The register gives it 05; the PDF carries no number.
 *
 * The words, the argument and the plan are the author's. Every figure that
 * somebody else published was checked against its source on 27 October 2026,
 * and every figure of the plan's own is computed on this page from the
 * model's inputs (src/lib/whey.ts) rather than typed. What changed:
 *   - "Ontario's modernization stream, up to $200,000, still taking
 *     applications." Ontario's guidelines say the Dairy Processing
 *     Modernization Initiative's intake closed on 13 February 2025. The
 *     page says so, and the "Us" column says a top-up only if a successor
 *     opens.
 *   - "On the order of 60,000 milligrams of oxygen demand per litre." FAO
 *     gives whey a BOD of 30 to 40 grams a litre and Pires et al. (2021)
 *     about 50 for second cheese whey; the page says 30,000 to 50,000 and
 *     more.
 *   - "Julien Chamberland ... told an Ontario symposium that in 2024." The
 *     Farmtario story quotes him on the equipment and summarises his team's
 *     research as finding feed the only viable option for very small
 *     plants; the feed line is the writer's framing. The page attributes it
 *     to his research, and names the 2026 paper (Kpadé, Chamberland, Singbo,
 *     Pouliot and Doyen, Waste and Biomass Valorization 17:8127).
 *   - "One pallet. About a tonne." Suppliers' pallets of WPC80 run from 600
 *     to 1,000 kg (30 to 40 sacks of 20 or 25 kg). The line is now "one
 *     tonne of powder", which is what the arithmetic is for.
 *   - "About nine kilograms of whey" for every kilogram of cheese: Pires et
 *     al. give 9 to 10 litres; the page says nine or ten litres.
 *   - "About US$3.70 a pound, 2024 to 2025": no published series states it.
 *     CNN (27 September 2026, on USDA data) reports June's US$13 as a 250
 *     per cent rise year over year, which puts mid-2025 near US$3.70; the
 *     page says so and cites that.
 *   - "Payback under 6 months" in the downside case. The inputs put it at
 *     6.0 months; the page prints the computed months in every case
 *     (6.0, 4.5 and 2.7).
 *   - "Volume down more than 96%" for the retentate. From 100,000 litres to
 *     about 3,000 is 97%; printed computed.
 *   - "About a third of that landed price" for $17.50 against $46 to $51 is
 *     34 to 38 per cent; the page says "a little over a third".
 *   - The surtax arithmetic is shown as the page computes it, on the value
 *     for duty, and the page says so; the PDF's "$46 to $51" holds.
 *   - SMPIF's "licence and the history": the fund asks for two complete
 *     financial statements covering three years of revenue, with at least
 *     24 months of processing revenue; the page says that.
 *   - The US side: the 50 per cent duty is Proclamation 11047 (signed 20
 *     July 2026, in effect from 22 August after a three-day suspension
 *     lapsed) and the prohibition is Proclamation 11062 (8 September 2026,
 *     effective 29 September), both cited. The "$20 billion" some coverage
 *     attaches to the US duty is the size of Canada's own package.
 * Could not be verified from this sandbox and stand as the author's cited
 * claims, flagged on the page: the two Vesper tonne prices (US$28,160 in
 * August 2026 against US$5,620 in August 2023), which the PDF cites to the
 * Financial Post of 21 September 2026 and which no reachable outlet repeats
 * (financialpost.com refuses fetches; the Post's own post of the story
 * confirms the title, the date and the 90 per cent); and the byline
 * (Andrew Rankin), which no reachable copy names. Verified as written: the
 * 90 per cent ("about 90% of it from the U.S."), the Customs Notice (number,
 * date, 50 per cent in Schedule 3 of SOR/2026-186 on 0404.10 and 0404.90,
 * cheese at 25), the 29 September prohibition, the June peak and the late-
 * September range (USDA via CNN; USDA's own weekly report for 19 to 23
 * October has WPC 80 "in the $9s and $10s"), the Bank of Canada averages
 * (1.3698 and 1.3978), SMPIF's cap, budget, end date and Stage 1 address,
 * AgriInnovate's status and $5 million, Protein Industries Canada's mandate
 * and its 27 May 2026 brewery project, Mark Hamel's billion litres, the Laval
 * study, section 247 and the Health Canada boundary, the BC fund's 2024
 * close and its October 2026 renewal notice.
 *
 * ADDED for its stakeholders, as on the last three papers, and listed here
 * so the owner can strike any: the "In brief" box, the "How to read this"
 * note, the "Carry it" section (one line to each desk the paper names,
 * each a mailto to the site's address with the desk in the subject, the
 * same processor-free pattern as /join; no form, no list of names, no
 * endorsement implied) and the "Words used here" glossary. Every other
 * sentence is the PDF's, with the figures rendered from the model or from
 * figures.ts.
 *
 * THE OPENING was turned round on 27 October 2026 at the owner's ask. The
 * PDF opens on the loop: whey shipped south and bought back, 90 per cent
 * from the United States, which puts Canada second in its own first
 * sentence. The page now opens on what Canada already holds: the whey is
 * made, the milk is paid, the plant is licensed; the plan; the day; the
 * year. The border comes fourth, as weather, and the loop section's
 * heading ("We already milked it") already said so. The facts row leads
 * with the nine litres and the skid; the surtax is third. Not a word of
 * the author's argument changed; its order did.
 *
 * FORM. Four diagrams (Figures 1, 4, 5 and 8) follow the site's diagram
 * discipline (CLAUDE.md): one accent on one focal element, a four-pixel
 * grid, orthogonal connectors, hairlines, square corners, no colour carrying
 * meaning a label does not. Figures 2, 3, 6 and 7 are tables first (BarTable
 * or a plain table), because they are numbers. The PDF's price pair, landed
 * price, ledger and skid table had been laid out as slides; they are tables
 * here so every value has a row and column header.
 *
 * INTERACTIVE, added 27 October 2026 at the owner's ask. Two panels run the
 * paper's own arithmetic in the reader's browser: "Build the landed price"
 * (a US price, an exchange rate, the surtax on or off) and "Run it yourself"
 * (whey a day, price, yield, grant share, toll). Both import src/lib/whey.ts,
 * the same module the page ran at build, so the browser's numbers and the
 * printed ones cannot disagree; the base case is rendered at build, so a
 * reader without scripting sees the ledger's own figures and inert sliders.
 * The surtax rate reaches the script from the markup (data-surtax), never
 * from the script's bytes. Figure 1's two paths carry a moving dash so the
 * loop reads as a loop; it stops under prefers-reduced-motion. Figure 4
 * gains a strip to scale, because "mostly water" is a claim the eye should
 * be able to check: 95 of every 100 litres leave as permeate.
 */
const d = day();
const c = cases();
const pl = plant();
const fx = 1.40;
const surtax = WHEY_SURTAX.value / 100;
const lo = landed(10, fx, surtax);
const hi = landed(11, fx, surtax);
const skid = skidTotal();
const grant = grantAmount();
const net = netCapital();
const months = (m: number) => `${m.toFixed(1)} months`;
const m = dollars;
const cap = (t: string) => t[0].toUpperCase() + t.slice(1);
/** "$2.8 million", for prose; the tables print the dollar. */
const millions = (v: number) => `$${(v / 1_000_000).toFixed(1)} million`;
// The "run it yourself" panel renders the base case at build, so a reader
// without scripting sees the ledger's own numbers; the script re-runs the
// same run() when an input moves.
const r0 = run(BASE_INPUTS);
const pct = (v: number) => `${Math.max(0, v).toFixed(2)}%`;
const litres = (v: number) => `${v.toLocaleString('en-CA')} L`;
// The landed-price builder's bar is scaled to the top of its own slider, so
// it can never overflow: US$15 a pound at $1.50 with the surtax.
const LAND_MAX = landed(15, 1.5, surtax).withSurtax;
const strip = { permeate: (d.permeateLitres / MODEL.litresPerDay) * 352, retentate: (d.retentateLitres / MODEL.litresPerDay) * 352, cream: (d.creamKg / MODEL.litresPerDay) * 352 };
---
<Read
  slug="the-protein-leaves-as-water"
  pageTitle="The Protein Leaves as Water"
  description={`Canada ships its whey south as water, buys the powder back, and since 8 September 2026 pays a ${WHEY_SURTAX.display} surtax on the return. A plan to dry Canadian whey in Canada: one skid beside one vat, not a new factory. Every figure named.`}
  standfirst={"A plan to dry Canadian whey in Canada. Not a new factory. One skid, beside a vat that already has the milk, the licence and the waste."}
>
      <section class="brief" aria-labelledby="in-brief">
        <h2 id="in-brief">In brief</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Canada already makes the protein. Every kilogram of Canadian cheese leaves nine or ten litres of whey behind, and the protein is in the nine. The milk is paid for. The plant is licensed. Nothing new has to be grown.</li>
          <li>The plan is one skid beside one vat: about {millions(MODEL.capex)} installed, on the floor of a plant that already has the licence, the milk and the waste. Not a new factory. Not a new cow. Not a new quota.</li>
          <li>One skid on one day: {kg(MODEL.litresPerDay).replace(' kg', ' litres')} of sweet whey in; {kg(d.creamKg)} of cream sold first, about {MODEL.retentateLitres.toLocaleString('en-CA')} litres of protein to a dryer we rent, and the water back to the plant. {kg(MODEL.basePowderKgDay)} of powder a day in the cautious case.</li>
          <li>The year returns about {millions(c.base.ebitda)} before interest and tax in the base case. Cream alone covers drying, freight and power. At $14 a kilogram it still clears about {millions(c.downside.ebitda)}.</li>
          <li>Until now that whey left as water and came back as powder: {WHEY_US_SHARE.display} of the high-protein whey Canada imports has been American. In September both doors closed in one month: a {WHEY_SURTAX.display} surtax on the return from 8 September, and listed Canadian whey stopped at the American border from the 29th. The loop no longer pays, which is the moment to stop running it.</li>
          <li>The money: one federal door is open to a licensed cheese plant, up to {SMPIF_CAP.display}, non-repayable. The plant-protein fund will say no, and the interest-free loan is closed. Eight desks can stop it, and the page says what each is shown.</li>
          <li>No cheese plant, dryer or brand has agreed to any of this. The sequence is the work.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>9 L</dt><dd>of whey for every kilogram of Canadian cheese. The protein is in the nine.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></dd></div>
        <div><dt>{m(MODEL.capex).replace(',800,000', '.8M')}</dt><dd>the skid we model, beside a vat. Not a new factory.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>{WHEY_SURTAX.display}</dt><dd>surtax on the powder we have been buying back, since 8 September 2026.<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></dd></div>
      </dl>

      <h3>How to read this</h3>
      <p>Every published figure here is dated and sourced in the receipts at the end. Every figure of the plan’s own is a planning model, October 2026, and the page says so wherever one appears; the model’s inputs are written once in the site’s source, every number that follows from them is computed on this page rather than typed, and the <a href="#words">words used here</a> explain the terms.</p>
      <p>It is written for the people it would need: a cheese plant, a dryer, a blender, the dairy desk at Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada, a milk board, and anyone with a corner of a floor and a reason.</p>
      <p class="byline">Northern Temper, October 2026. A planning model, not a term sheet. <a href="#sources">The receipts are at the end.</a></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">01 · The loop</span> We already milked it.</h2>
      <figure class="diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 400 248" role="img" aria-labelledby="lp-t lp-d">
          <title id="lp-t">The loop</title>
          <desc id="lp-d">A Canadian cheese vat separates curd from sweet whey. The whey, mostly water, travels south to be dried in the United States. The powder travels back north to a Canadian blender, and at the border on the way back it now carries a fifty per cent surtax.</desc>
          <text x="8" y="20" class="eyebrow-t">CANADA</text>
          <text x="8" y="240" class="eyebrow-t">UNITED STATES</text>
          <line x1="8" y1="124" x2="392" y2="124" class="hair" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/>
          <rect x="24" y="32" width="128" height="48" class="box"/>
          <text x="88" y="52" text-anchor="middle" class="name">THE VAT</text>
          <text x="88" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">sweet whey, mostly water</text>
          <rect x="248" y="32" width="128" height="48" class="box"/>
          <text x="312" y="52" text-anchor="middle" class="name">THE BLENDER</text>
          <text x="312" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">powder, bought back</text>
          <rect x="136" y="168" width="128" height="48" class="box"/>
          <text x="200" y="188" text-anchor="middle" class="name">A U.S. DRYER</text>
          <text x="200" y="204" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">not ours</text>
          <path d="M88 80 V192 H128" class="line flow" fill="none"/>
          <polygon points="128,188 136,192 128,196" class="head"/>
          <text x="96" y="156" class="sub">whey, south, as water</text>
          <path d="M264 192 H312 V88" class="line flow" fill="none"/>
          <polygon points="308,88 312,80 316,88" class="head"/>
          <rect x="272" y="108" width="80" height="32" class="box focal"/>
          <text x="312" y="128" text-anchor="middle" class="name focal-t">{WHEY_SURTAX.display} SURTAX</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Figure 1 · The loop. The milk was already bought. The protein leaves as water and comes back as powder, and since 8 September 2026 the return is taxed.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a>, <a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <dl class="steps">
        <div><dt>Vat</dt><dd>A Canadian cheese plant separates curd from sweet whey. The milk was already bought. No new cow. No new quota.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>South</dt><dd>The whey, mostly water, has been shipped to be dried in the United States, then sold back to Canada as powder.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Back</dt><dd>{cap(WHEY_US_SHARE.display)} of the high-protein whey Canada imports still comes from the United States.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="pull">The return is now taxed. Since 8 September 2026 that powder carries a {WHEY_SURTAX.display} surtax. The loop no longer pays.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">02 · The border</span> Both doors closed in September.</h2>
      <div class="compare">
        <article>
          <p class="big">8 Sept</p>
          <h3>Canada</h3>
          <p>A {WHEY_SURTAX.display} surtax on United States whey. Customs Notice 26-23.<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="big">29 Sept</p>
          <h3>United States</h3>
          <p>Listed Canadian whey stopped at the border.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <BarTable name="Figure 2" rowHead="Average US whey protein concentrate" columns={['US dollars a tonne']} unit="usd" rows={[
        { label: WPC_AUG_2023.period, values: [WPC_AUG_2023.value] },
        { label: WPC_AUG_2026.period, values: [WPC_AUG_2026.value] },
      ]}>Figure 2 · Vesper, cited by the Financial Post. {WPC_AUG_2026.period} is {WPC_RATIO.toFixed(1)} times {WPC_AUG_2023.period}. The surtax sits on top of that price.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup></BarTable>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">03 · The penalty</span> What a kilogram costs now.</h2>
      <section class="tw" tabindex="0" aria-label="Before and this autumn, scrolls sideways">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Line</th> <th scope="col">Before</th> <th scope="col">This autumn</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">US wholesale</th> <td>About US$3.70 a pound in mid-2025, implied by the 250% rise to June.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup></td> <td>US$10 to $11 a pound. June peaked at US$13.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Surtax, HS 0404</th> <td>None under CUSMA.</td> <td>{WHEY_SURTAX.display} since 8 September 2026.<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">The US door</th> <td>Canadian whey could enter.</td> <td>Listed powders stopped, 29 September.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Planning rate</th> <td>$1.37 in 2024.</td> <td>${fx.toFixed(2)}.<sup><a href="#note-4">4</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Landed, our sum</th> <td>Duty-free.</td> <td>About {m(lo.withSurtax).replace(/\.\d+$/, '')} to {m(hi.withSurtax).replace(/\.\d+$/, '')} a kilogram, Canadian, before freight.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">What we model</th> <td></td> <td>${MODEL.pricePerKg.toFixed(2)} a kilogram. A little over a third of that landed price.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">One tonne of powder</th> <td></td> <td>Roughly {m(Math.floor(lo.surtaxPerKg * 1000 / 1000) * 1000)} of the bill is the surtax alone.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
      <BarTable name="Figure 3" rowHead="A kilogram of US powder" columns={['At US$10 a pound', 'At US$11 a pound']} unit="cents" rows={[
        { label: `In Canadian dollars, at $${fx.toFixed(2)}`, values: [Math.round(lo.cadPerKg * 100) / 100, Math.round(hi.cadPerKg * 100) / 100] },
        { label: `With the ${WHEY_SURTAX.display} surtax`, values: [Math.round(lo.withSurtax * 100) / 100, Math.round(hi.withSurtax * 100) / 100] },
        { label: 'What we model, Canadian powder', values: [MODEL.pricePerKg, MODEL.pricePerKg] },
      ]}>Figure 3 · The worked line, late-September low: US$10 a pound is US${lo.usdPerKg.toFixed(2)} a kilogram, times ${fx.toFixed(2)} is ${lo.cadPerKg.toFixed(2)}, times the {WHEY_SURTAX.display} surtax is ${lo.withSurtax.toFixed(2)} Canadian. The surtax is charged on the value for duty, so this is the low end of the bill. The ${MODEL.pricePerKg.toFixed(2)} is our asking price in the model, not a contract.</BarTable>
      <section class="panel" id="land" aria-labelledby="land-h">
        <h3 id="land-h"><span class="eyebrow">Try it</span> Build the landed price.</h3>
        <p>Move the American price and the exchange rate, and switch the surtax off to see what it alone adds. The black mark is the ${MODEL.pricePerKg.toFixed(2)} the model asks for Canadian powder.</p>
        <div class="ctls" role="group" aria-labelledby="land-h" data-land data-surtax={surtax} data-max={LAND_MAX} data-ours={MODEL.pricePerKg}>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label for="in-usd">US price, a pound</label>
            <input type="range" id="in-usd" name="usd" min="3" max="15" step="0.25" value="10" />
            <output for="in-usd" data-echo="usd">US$10.00</output>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl">
            <label for="in-fx">One US dollar, in Canadian dollars</label>
            <input type="range" id="in-fx" name="fx" min="1.25" max="1.5" step="0.01" value={fx} />
            <output for="in-fx" data-echo="fx">${fx.toFixed(2)}</output>
          </div>
          <div class="ctl check">
            <input type="checkbox" id="in-surtax" name="surtax" checked />
            <label for="in-surtax">With the {WHEY_SURTAX.display} surtax</label>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lbar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="seg base" data-seg="base" style={`width:${pct((lo.cadPerKg / LAND_MAX) * 100)}`}></span><span class="seg surtax" data-seg="surtax" style={`width:${pct((lo.surtaxPerKg / LAND_MAX) * 100)}`}></span>
          <span class="ours" style={`left:${pct((MODEL.pricePerKg / LAND_MAX) * 100)}`}></span>
        </div>
        <dl class="facts three">
          <div><dt data-out="landed">${lo.withSurtax.toFixed(2)}</dt><dd>a kilogram, landed in Canada, before freight</dd></div>
          <div><dt data-out="surtax">${lo.surtaxPerKg.toFixed(2)}</dt><dd>of that is the surtax</dd></div>
          <div><dt data-out="ours">{((MODEL.pricePerKg / lo.withSurtax) * 100).toFixed(0)}%</dt><dd>is where ${MODEL.pricePerKg.toFixed(2)}, the model’s Canadian price, sits against it</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <noscript><p class="fig">Moving the inputs needs scripting. The late-September low is shown.</p></noscript>
      </section>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">04 · Not this. This.</span> Do not build a factory. Sit beside the vat.</h2>
      <div class="compare">
        <article>
          <h3>Not this</h3>
          <p>Raw whey is about 93% water. It spoils in hours. A tanker of it is a tanker of water.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></p>
          <p>A greenfield spray dryer is the $15 to $30 million machine, inside a $50 to $100 million plant, and two or three years of permits.</p>
          <p class="big">$50–100M<small>And two or three years of permits.</small></p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>This</h3>
          <p>Phase one is a skid on the floor of a plant that already has the licence, the milk, and the waste.</p>
          <p>Sweet whey only. Cheddar, mozzarella, gouda. pH 6.0 to 6.5. Acid whey from cottage cheese or Greek yogurt is refused. It scales the membranes.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></p>
          <p class="big">{m(skid.low).replace(',100,000', '.1')}–{m(skid.high).replace(',200,000', '.2M')}<small>We model {m(MODEL.capex).replace(',800,000', '.8M')}.</small></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">05 · One day</span> One hundred thousand litres.</h2>
      <figure class="diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 400 304" role="img" aria-labelledby="md-t md-d">
          <title id="md-t">One day through the skid</title>
          <desc id="md-d">One hundred thousand litres of sweet whey enter the skid. A strip to scale shows that about ninety-five parts in a hundred leave as permeate, three as protein retentate and under one as cream. The clarifier takes off {kg(d.creamKg)} of forty per cent cream, sold first. About {MODEL.permeateLitres.toLocaleString('en-CA')} litres of permeate, lactose and minerals in water, go back to the plant. About {MODEL.retentateLitres.toLocaleString('en-CA')} litres of protein retentate are hauled cold to a rented dryer and become {kg(MODEL.basePowderKgDay)} of powder a day in the cautious case.</desc>
          <rect x="8" y="8" width="384" height="48" class="box"/>
          <text x="200" y="28" text-anchor="middle" class="name">{MODEL.litresPerDay.toLocaleString('en-CA')} L SWEET WHEY IN</text>
          <text x="200" y="44" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">a day, {MODEL.days} days a year</text>
          <rect x="40" y="60" width="352" height="6" class="track"/>
          <rect x="40" y="60" width={strip.permeate.toFixed(1)} height="6" class="sw-p"/>
          <rect x={(40 + strip.permeate).toFixed(1)} y="60" width={strip.retentate.toFixed(1)} height="6" class="sw-r"/>
          <rect x={(40 + strip.permeate + strip.retentate).toFixed(1)} y="60" width={Math.max(2, strip.cream).toFixed(1)} height="6" class="sw-c"/>
          <path d="M24 56 V196 M24 92 H32 M24 144 H32 M24 196 H32" class="line" fill="none"/>
          <polygon points="32,88 40,92 32,96" class="head"/>
          <polygon points="32,140 40,144 32,148" class="head"/>
          <polygon points="32,192 40,196 32,200" class="head"/>
          <rect x="40" y="72" width="352" height="40" class="box"/>
          <rect x="376" y="80" width="8" height="8" class="sw-c"/>
