      <h2><span class="eyebrow">07 · The plant</span> What the cheese plant is paid.</h2>
      <div class="compare">
        <article>
          <h3>$0 a litre up front</h3>
          <p>The whey is not purchased. It is the stream the plant already separates, and used to pay to haul away.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Hot water back</h3>
          <p>Polished water returns warm, into the plant’s own wash. Less city water. Less gas.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>The cream first</h3>
          <p>{m(c.base.cream)} a year from whey cream, before a kilogram of powder is sold. It covers drying, freight, and most of the power.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>{pl.centsPerLitre.toFixed(1)}¢ a litre after</h3>
          <p>On the base case, {Math.round(MODEL.plantShare * 100)}% of what is left goes back to the member plant. About {m(Math.round(pl.share / 1000) * 1000)} a year. A split in the model, not a promise.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="fig">{(pl.litres / 1_000_000).toFixed(0)} million litres a year. {m(pl.share)} is {Math.round(MODEL.plantShare * 100)}% of the {m(c.base.left)} left after costs and the loan in the base case. The other {Math.round((1 - MODEL.plantShare) * 100)}% is a reserve and the operator’s share. Nothing here is agreed.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">08 · The skid</span> Where the {m(MODEL.capex).replace(',800,000', '.8 million')} goes.</h2>
      <BarTable name="Figure 6" rowHead="Line" columns={['Low', 'High']} unit="dollars" rows={[
        ...MODEL.skid.map((r) => ({ label: r.label, values: [r.low, r.high] })),
        { label: 'Installed, no spray dryer', values: [skid.low, skid.high], bars: false },
      ]}>Figure 6 · Ranges are equipment estimates for a {MODEL.litresPerDay.toLocaleString('en-CA')}-litre-a-day loop, October 2026. The {m(MODEL.capex).replace(',800,000', '.8 million')} is the middle.</BarTable>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>{m(grant).replace(',000', 'k')}</dt><dd>Only if SMPIF covers {Math.round(MODEL.grantShare * 100)}%. The cheese plant has to apply. That share is our ask, not a rate the program publishes.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Closed</dt><dd>AgriInnovate is the interest-free loan. Its intake is shut. This model no longer spends that money.<sup><a href="#note-6">6</a></sup></dd></div>
        <div><dt>{m(net).replace(',000', 'k')}</dt><dd>What is actually left. Members, then a lender against a purchase order. Interest is not in the EBITDA.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="fig">Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada lists AgriInnovate as closed. Repayment, when the program is open, is interest-free. It is not open.<sup><a href="#note-6">6</a></sup></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">09 · Where it goes</span> Feed, or a sewer. Not a powder.</h2>
      <div class="compare">
        <article>
          <h3>Nine litres</h3>
          <p>Nine or ten litres of whey leave the vat for every kilogram of cheese. The milk was already paid for. The protein is in the nine, not the one.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>The small plant sells it as feed</h3>
          <p>For the plant without a dryer, livestock feed is the option that clears. Research by Julien Chamberland’s team at Université Laval, presented at an Ontario symposium in 2024, found it the only viable option for the smallest plants. The same group’s 2026 Quebec study found the same thing.<sup><a href="#note-8">8</a>, <a href="#note-9">9</a></sup></p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>The surplus is not small</h3>
          <p>Dairy Farmers of Ontario’s chair said the province needed another billion litres of processing capacity for the solids-not-fat surplus. That is capacity, not a count of litres poured out.<sup><a href="#note-8">8</a></sup></p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>When feed will not take it</h3>
          <p>Cheese whey carries 30,000 to 50,000 milligrams of oxygen demand per litre, and more. A municipal sewer is built for a few hundred. We will not claim a national figure for how much is flushed. The failure mode is the drain.<sup><a href="#note-10">10</a></sup></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">10 · The money</span> One door is open. One will say no.</h2>
      <section class="tw" tabindex="0" aria-label="The funding doors, scrolls sideways">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Door</th> <th scope="col">What it actually funds</th> <th scope="col">Us</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">SMPIF</th> <td>Dairy processors. New equipment. Non-repayable, up to {SMPIF_CAP.display}. Intake open. Ends 31 March 2028.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></td> <td>Yes, if the cheese plant applies. A new company cannot.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Protein Industries Canada</th> <td>Plant protein. Its own line is “a global leader in plant protein.” Consortium, up to about 45%. It has funded a brewery byproduct.<sup><a href="#note-11">11</a></sup></td> <td>No. We do not qualify, and we will not pretend to. The argument is that the policy stops at the plant door.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">AgriInnovate</th> <td>Repayable. Interest-free when it is open. Normally up to $5 million.<sup><a href="#note-6">6</a></sup></td> <td>Closed. The earlier stack that leaned on it is withdrawn.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Provincial dairy funds</th> <td>Ontario’s modernization stream paid up to $200,000 a plant. Its intake closed 13 February 2025. Real money, when it is open. Not a skid.<sup><a href="#note-12">12</a></sup></td> <td>A top-up if a successor opens, after the federal file. Not the plan.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">BC Manufacturing Jobs Fund</th> <td>Capital for B.C. manufacturers, when an intake is open: up to 20% of a project, $100,000 to $10 million.<sup><a href="#note-13">13</a></sup></td> <td>The last capital intake closed in 2024. A $30 million renewal was announced in October 2026 with no intake date. Watch it. Do not book it.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">11 · Who can stop it</span> Eight desks. Any one of them.</h2>
      <section class="tw" tabindex="0" aria-label="Eight desks, scrolls sideways">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Who</th> <th scope="col">How they stop us</th> <th scope="col">What we put in front of them</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">The cheese plant</th> <td>No letter, no whey, no file.</td> <td>Nothing up front. Hot water back. A share of the year, if the year makes money.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">The milk board</th> <td>If this looks like milk moving around the board.</td> <td>No new cow. No new quota. Whey only, after the vat. In B.C., the BC Milk Marketing Board. In Ontario, Dairy Farmers of Ontario.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">The Dairy Commission</th> <td>If the solids get reclassified and the pool objects.</td> <td>The milk was already paid as cheese milk. We are not buying a new class.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">CFIA</th> <td>A new activity that is not on the licence or the preventive control plan.</td> <td>The plant amends its plan. Bulk sacks under section 247. Not a new food company.<sup><a href="#note-14">14</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Health Canada</th> <td>A tub that claims to build muscle. That is a natural health product, and it needs a number before sale.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></td> <td>We do not sell a tub. The buyer already holds the product licence.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Environment, and the sewer</th> <td>Permeate dumped, or a new outfall. Lactose still has an oxygen demand.</td> <td>Protein and cream leave the drain. Lactose goes to a feed or fermentation buyer. Recovered water is reuse, not a discharge.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">The blender</th> <td>No purchase order, no lender.</td> <td>A certificate of analysis first. Then a take-or-pay. Not a handshake.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Our own members</th> <td>If we treat a closed program as cash.</td> <td>{m(net)} has to be found without AgriInnovate. Some of it from us.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
      <p class="fig">Safe Food for Canadians Regulations, section 247, is the bulk-dairy label. The natural health product line is Health Canada’s boundary, not a slogan. Nothing on this page is a consent from any of these desks.<sup><a href="#note-14">14</a>, <a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">12 · Who can file</span> The cheese plant applies.</h2>
      <ol class="file">
        <li><h3>The plant</h3><p>A licensed dairy processor, with books. A new company cannot carry this file. The fund wants the licence and the history: two complete financial statements covering three years of revenue, at least two of them from processing.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></p></li>
        <li><h3>Stage 1</h3><p>A project summary to the dairy desk at Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada. Non-repayable, up to {SMPIF_CAP.display}, program open to 31 March 2028. An application is not an approval.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></p></li>
        <li><h3>The skid</h3><p>We bring the equipment, the offtake, and the reason the file is worth writing. Installed capital in the model, {m(MODEL.capex)}.</p></li>
        <li><h3>The buyer</h3><p>Bulk powder to a Canadian blender who already holds a product licence. Not a claim on a tub. Not a school program. Plant-protein funds do not cover dairy whey.<sup><a href="#note-11">11</a></sup></p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="fig">Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada, Supply Management Processing Investment Fund. Dairy intake open. Budget {SMPIF_BUDGET.display}. Stage 1: <a href="mailto:aafc.smpif-fitpgo.aac@agr.gc.ca">aafc.smpif-fitpgo.aac@agr.gc.ca</a>.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">13 · The ask</span> Fund the equipment that stops a waste and a surtax.</h2>
      <div class="compare three">
        <article>
          <p class="big">01</p>
          <h3>One plant</h3>
          <p>A licensed cheese plant, already separating sweet whey, willing to be the applicant.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="big">02</p>
          <h3>One skid</h3>
          <p>Beside the vat. Not a new factory. Not a new cow. Not a new quota.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="big">03</p>
          <h3>One powder</h3>
          <p>Sold in Canada, as an ingredient. Schools and the Forces can buy it once a plant is actually making it.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="pull">We are not asking for a set-aside. We are asking that protein we already milked be dried here, instead of poured out and bought back.</p>
      <h3 id="carry"><span class="eyebrow">Carry it</span> One line to each desk.</h3>
      <p>This plan needs a few people who already hold a piece of it. If one of these is your desk, the link opens a message to us with the desk in the subject line. Nothing else is collected, and nobody’s name goes on a list.</p>
      <ul class="carry">
        <li><a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Whey · a cheese plant')}`}>A cheese plant</a> separating sweet whey, with a corner of floor and a reason. The first letter is yours to write.</li>
        <li><a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Whey · a dryer')}`}>A dryer</a> with a spray tower and a toll rate. A term sheet before anyone welds a frame.</li>
        <li><a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Whey · a blender')}`}>A blender</a> holding a product licence, who would read a certificate of analysis and talk about a take-or-pay.</li>
        <li><a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Whey · a milk board or the Dairy Commission')}`}>A milk board, or the Dairy Commission</a>, who can say early whether whey after the vat is anything the pool minds.</li>
        <li><a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Whey · the dairy desk')}`}>The dairy desk</a> at Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada, if the file reads as one worth a Stage 1.</li>
        <li><a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Whey · what breaks first')}`}>Anyone who knows what breaks first</a>. If a number above is wrong, that is the message we most want.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">14 · Twelve months</span> The order of work.</h2>
      <figure class="diagram">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 400 204" role="img" aria-labelledby="tm-t tm-d">
          <title id="tm-t">Twelve months, in order</title>
          <desc id="tm-d">A twelve-month timeline in four steps. Months one and two: a plant says yes. Months three and four: a pilot powder, then the Stage 1 file. Months five to eight: a take-or-pay, then the skid is built. Months nine to twelve: tie-in, licence, first sacks. The first step is the one everything waits on.</desc>
          <path d="M8 16 V176 M40 16 V176 M72 16 V176 M104 16 V176 M136 16 V176 M168 16 V176 M200 16 V176 M232 16 V176 M264 16 V176 M296 16 V176 M328 16 V176 M360 16 V176 M392 16 V176" class="grid" fill="none"/>
          <text x="8" y="24" class="name focal-t">A PLANT SAYS YES</text>
          <rect x="8" y="28" width="64" height="24" class="box focal"/>
          <text x="72" y="64" class="name">PILOT POWDER, THEN THE FILE</text>
          <rect x="72" y="68" width="64" height="24" class="box"/>
          <text x="136" y="104" class="name">TAKE-OR-PAY, THEN BUILD</text>
          <rect x="136" y="108" width="128" height="24" class="box"/>
          <text x="392" y="144" text-anchor="end" class="name">TIE-IN, LICENCE, FIRST SACKS</text>
          <rect x="264" y="148" width="128" height="24" class="box"/>
          <text x="8" y="192" class="eyebrow-t">MONTH 1</text>
          <text x="104" y="192" text-anchor="middle" class="eyebrow-t">4</text>
          <text x="232" y="192" text-anchor="middle" class="eyebrow-t">8</text>
