        <figcaption>Figure 7 · The order of work. No cheese plant, dryer, or brand has agreed to any of this. The sequence is the work. It is not a list of partners.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <dl class="phases">
        <dt>Months 1 to 2</dt>
        <dd>Three directors. A letter from one host plant for about {MODEL.litresPerDay.toLocaleString('en-CA')} litres a day and a corner of the floor. Five litres of sweet whey, cold, the same week. No co-op until a plant says yes.</dd>
        <dt>Months 3 to 4</dt>
        <dd>A pilot powder and a certificate of analysis. Then the Stage 1 summary. A toll-dry term sheet before anyone welds a frame.</dd>
        <dt>Months 5 to 8</dt>
        <dd>A take-or-pay from one blender, about $16.50 to ${MODEL.pricePerKg.toFixed(2)} a kilogram, 24 months. That contract is what a lender can read. Then the skid is built.</dd>
        <dt>Months 9 to 12</dt>
        <dd>Stainless into the existing vat. The preventive control plan. Bulk sacks, not a retail tub. Cream one way. Retentate the other. Powder back.</dd>
      </dl>
      <figure class="mark">
        <BearDual />
      </figure>
      <p class="sign">Northern Temper</p>
      <p>October 2026</p>

      <h2 id="words"><span class="eyebrow">Words used here</span> Nine terms, plainly.</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>Sweet whey</dt>
        <dd>The liquid left when rennet sets milk into curd for cheddar, mozzarella or gouda. About 93 to 94% water, with a little under 1% true protein. Acid whey, from cottage cheese or Greek yogurt, is more acid and higher in minerals, and it fouls membranes; the plan refuses it.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Clarifier and cream separator</dt>
        <dd>A centrifuge that spins out curd fines and most of the fat before the whey reaches the membranes. The fat leaves as 40% cream, which is the first thing sold.</dd>
        <dt>Ultrafiltration</dt>
        <dd>Pushing whey through a membrane that holds back the large protein molecules and lets water, lactose and salts through. What stays is the retentate; what passes is the permeate. Diafiltration adds clean water to wash more lactose out.</dd>
        <dt>Retentate</dt>
        <dd>The protein side of the membrane: about {MODEL.retentateLitres.toLocaleString('en-CA')} litres a day at 25% solids, from {MODEL.litresPerDay.toLocaleString('en-CA')} in. Small enough to truck cold to a dryer.</dd>
        <dt>Toll drying</dt>
        <dd>Paying a plant that already owns a spray dryer to dry your retentate into powder, by the kilogram. The model pays ${MODEL.tollPerKg.toFixed(2)} a kilogram rather than buying a $15 to $30 million dryer.</dd>
        <dt>Take-or-pay</dt>
        <dd>A contract where the buyer pays for an agreed quantity whether or not they take it. A lender can finance equipment against one; a handshake they cannot read.</dd>
        <dt>Surtax</dt>
        <dd>A duty added on top of the normal tariff, by order, as a countermeasure. Canada’s on United States whey is {WHEY_SURTAX.display} of the value for duty, from 8 September 2026.<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>EBITDA</dt>
        <dd>Earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation: what the year leaves after operating costs and before the cost of the money that built it.</dd>
        <dt>SMPIF</dt>
        <dd>The Supply Management Processing Investment Fund, Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada’s non-repayable fund for dairy, poultry and egg processors buying equipment. Up to {SMPIF_CAP.display} a project; the dairy intake is open until the {SMPIF_BUDGET.display} is committed or 31 March 2028.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a></sup></dd>
      </dl>

      <h2 id="sources"><span class="eyebrow">Sources</span> Every figure, named.</h2>
      <ol class="notes">
        <li id="note-1"><cite>Financial Post</cite>, &quot;How Canada&#x27;s 50% whey tariff is backfiring on Canadian protein makers,&quot; 21 September 2026 (the PDF credits Andrew Rankin): &quot;Canada imports roughly 1,000 tonnes of high-protein whey a month, about 90% of it from the U.S.&quot;; Canadian production of WPC80 and isolate is &quot;next to none&quot; beside the American; and &quot;Beginning Sept. 29, the U.S. will prohibit imports of specified Canadian whey.&quot; The prohibition is Proclamation 11062 of 8 September 2026, effective for goods imported on or after 29 September, whose annex lists whey protein concentrates, modified whey, fluid whey and dried whey; the 50 per cent American duty on Canadian dairy that preceded it is Proclamation 11047, signed 20 July and in effect from 22 August 2026. <a href="https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/09/excluding-certain-canadian-products-from-importation-into-the-united-states-in-response-to-continued-discrimination-against-the-commerce-of-the-united-states-with-respect-to-dairy/">whitehouse.gov</a> · <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/DCPD-202600581/html/DCPD-202600581.htm">govinfo.gov</a></li>
        <li id="note-2">Canada Border Services Agency, <cite>Customs Notice 26-23: United States Surtax Order (2026)</cite>, Ottawa, 7 September 2026: &quot;Effective September 8, 2026,&quot; goods in schedule 3 carry a surtax of &quot;50% of the value for duty.&quot; <a href="https://www.cbsa-asfc.gc.ca/publications/cn-ad/cn26-23-eng.html">cbsa-asfc.gc.ca</a>. The Order, SOR/2026-186, <cite>Canada Gazette</cite> Part II, 23 September 2026: Schedule 3 lists 0404.10.10 to 0404.90.20, the whey lines, at 50 per cent; cheese under 0406 is in Schedule 2 at 25. <a href="https://gazette.gc.ca/rp-pr/p2/2026/2026-09-23/html/sor-dors186-eng.html">gazette.gc.ca</a></li>
        <li id="note-3">Vesper, as the <cite>Financial Post</cite> cited it (note 1): average United States price of whey protein concentrate, more than {WPC_AUG_2026.display} a tonne in {WPC_AUG_2026.period}, from about {WPC_AUG_2023.display} a tonne in {WPC_AUG_2023.period}. <strong>The Post&#x27;s page could not be opened from where this edition was prepared, and no other outlet repeats the two tonne prices, so they stand as the author&#x27;s cited claim.</strong> What is verified says the same thing: <cite>CNN</cite>, 27 September 2026, on USDA data, has WPC at a record US$13 a pound by June, a 250 per cent rise year over year, and &quot;around $10 to $11 a pound&quot; since (<a href="https://www.cnn.com/2026/09/27/business/whey-protein-trade-war">cnn.com</a>); USDA <cite>Dairy Market News</cite> for 19 to 23 October 2026 has WPC 80 &quot;in the $9s and $10s&quot; (<a href="https://www.ams.usda.gov/mnreports/dywweeklyreport.pdf">ams.usda.gov</a>); and <cite>NutraIngredients</cite>, 19 June 2026, citing Vesper, has WPC80 at US$32,000 a tonne against US$5,500 in the same period of 2023 (<a href="https://www.nutraingredients.com/Article/2026/06/19/protein-brands-grapple-with-soaring-whey-costs-amid-heightened-demand/">nutraingredients.com</a>). The &quot;about US$3.70&quot; before is the June price divided by that 250 per cent rise.</li>
        <li id="note-4">Bank of Canada, annual average exchange rates, US dollar in Canadian dollars: 1.3698 for 2024 and 1.3978 for 2025. The model&#x27;s planning rate is the 2025 average, rounded. <a href="https://www.bankofcanada.ca/rates/exchange/annual-average-exchange-rates/">bankofcanada.ca</a></li>
        <li id="note-5">Pires, Marnotes, Rubio, Garcia and Pereira, &quot;Dairy By-Products: A Review on the Valorization of Whey and Second Cheese Whey,&quot; <cite>Foods</cite> 10(5):1067, 2021: &quot;Around 9–10 L of whey results from the production of 1 kg of cheese,&quot; sweet whey at pH 6 to 7 and acid whey about 4.5 to 5.8. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8151190/">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov</a>. Tetra Pak, <cite>Dairy Processing Handbook</cite>, chapter 18, table 18.1: cheese whey about 94 per cent water, 6 per cent solids; sweet whey pH 5.9 to 6.6. <a href="https://dairyprocessinghandbook.tetrapak.com/chapter/milk-and-whey-fractionation">dairyprocessinghandbook.tetrapak.com</a>. The pH 6.0 to 6.5 the plan takes in is its own intake specification inside that range.</li>
        <li id="note-6">Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada, <cite>AgriInnovate Program</cite>: &quot;Intake period: Closed&quot;; contributions &quot;on a repayable basis,&quot; normally not above $5,000,000 a project; &quot;Interest is not charged on the contribution funding.&quot; <a href="https://agriculture.canada.ca/en/programs/agriinnovate">agriculture.canada.ca</a></li>
        <li id="note-7">Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada, <cite>Supply Management Processing Investment Fund</cite>: &quot;The application intake period for contribution funding is open for dairy processors&quot;; budget {SMPIF_BUDGET.display} from 1 April 2022 to 31 March 2028; up to {SMPIF_CAP.display} for a dairy processor, non-repayable; applicants hold &quot;an active federal or provincial processing licence&quot; and file &quot;2 complete Financial Statements (which will cover three years of revenues),&quot; with at least 24 months of processing revenue; Stage 1 is a Project Summary Form to aafc.smpif-fitpgo.aac@agr.gc.ca. <a href="https://agriculture.canada.ca/en/programs/supply-management-processing-investment-fund">agriculture.canada.ca</a>. Confirm the program with the department before it is quoted as an offer.</li>
        <li id="note-8"><cite>Farmtario</cite>, Stew Slater, &quot;Dairy processors are challenged to find viable options,&quot; 8 March 2024, from the Southwestern Ontario Dairy Symposium: Chamberland on the equipment whey protein takes, his team&#x27;s finding that for the very small plants &quot;continuing to send all whey into the animal feed stream appears to be the only viable option,&quot; and Mark Hamel of Dairy Farmers of Ontario on &quot;an additional billion litres in annual processing capacity&quot; for the province&#x27;s solids-not-fat surplus. <a href="https://farmtario.com/livestock/dairy-processors-are-challenged-to-find-viable-options/">farmtario.com</a></li>
        <li id="note-9">Kpadé, Chamberland, Singbo, Pouliot and Doyen, &quot;Evaluating Techno-Economic Performances of Solids Non-fat Valorization from Cheese By-products in the Province of Québec (Canada),&quot; <cite>Waste and Biomass Valorization</cite> 17:8127–8145, 2026: for small plants, liquid whey to animal feed is &quot;the only viable option.&quot; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s12649-026-03615-x">doi.org</a></li>
        <li id="note-10">FAO, Thivend, &quot;Use of whey in feeding ruminants,&quot; <cite>World Animal Review</cite>: whey has a &quot;biochemical oxygen demand (BOD5) ranging from 30 to 40 g of oxygen per litre&quot; (<a href="https://www.fao.org/4/x6512e/X6512E09.htm">fao.org</a>); Pires et al. (note 5) give about 50 g a litre for second cheese whey. Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources, 1998: ordinary dairy-plant wastewater at 2,000 to 3,000 mg/L is &quot;10 times the strength of domestic sewage&quot; (<a href="https://datcp.wi.gov/documents/wheyspreading.pdf">datcp.wi.gov</a>). A published process range, not a national inventory.</li>
        <li id="note-11">Protein Industries Canada: its vision is that &quot;Canada is a global leader in plant protein,&quot; projects &quot;must have a clear connection to plant protein,&quot; and co-investment is 45 per cent of a project&#x27;s costs for a consortium of three or more companies (<a href="https://www.proteinindustriescanada.ca/technology">proteinindustriescanada.ca</a>). &quot;Terra Bioindustries and Great Western Brewing Company partner,&quot; 27 May 2026: $1.1 million into a brewery byproduct, $486,000 of it from the cluster. <a href="https://www.proteinindustriescanada.ca/news-releases/terra-gwbc">proteinindustriescanada.ca</a></li>
        <li id="note-12">Ontario, <cite>Dairy Processing Modernization Initiative</cite> guidelines: at most $200,000 a plant across all projects; &quot;Application intake for this Initiative closed on February 13, 2025.&quot; <a href="https://www.ontario.ca/page/dairy-processing-modernization-initiative-guidelines">ontario.ca</a></li>
        <li id="note-13">Government of British Columbia, <cite>BC Manufacturing Jobs Fund</cite>: &quot;the program intake is currently closed&quot;; &quot;an additional $30M has been announced for a renewed BC Manufacturing Jobs Fund&quot; (page updated 6 October 2026); the June 2024 program guide set the capital stream at $100,000 to $10 million and up to 20 per cent of eligible costs, with the last intake closing in 2024. <a href="https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/employment-business/economic-development/support-organizations-community-partners/manufacturing-jobs-fund">www2.gov.bc.ca</a></li>
        <li id="note-14"><cite>Safe Food for Canadians Regulations</cite>, SOR/2018-108, section 247: &quot;Prepackaged dairy products — not consumer prepackaged.&quot; <a href="https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2018-108/section-247.html">laws-lois.justice.gc.ca</a>. Canadian Food Inspection Agency on whey cream in butter: <a href="https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-labels/labelling/industry/dairy/whey-cream">inspection.canada.ca</a></li>
        <li id="note-15">Health Canada, <cite>Classification of Products at the Food-Natural Health Product Interface: Products in Food Formats</cite>: powdered protein products represented with terms such as &quot;muscle building&quot; or sold in measured doses are typically natural health products (<a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/natural-non-prescription/legislation-guidelines/guidance-documents/classification-products-at-food-natural-health-product-interface.html">canada.ca</a>); <cite>Natural Health Products Regulations</cite>, section 4: no sale without a product licence (<a href="https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2003-196/section-4.html">laws-lois.justice.gc.ca</a>).</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="fig"><strong>A note on this edition.</strong> The skid cost, the daily yields, the ${MODEL.pricePerKg.toFixed(2)} price, the cream, the operating lines and the EBITDA are our planning model, October 2026, computed on this page from its inputs. Six details were corrected against their sources before this edition was published, among them a provincial fund the PDF called open that closed in February 2025; two prices the PDF cites to the Financial Post could not be verified from here and are marked as the author’s cited claim where they appear. Programs and prices move; check the current version of each source before any of this is quoted as an offer. The page’s own record of each change is in its source.</p>

